# Prehistoric India: The Stone Age Foundations

> Before kings, scripts, and cities, the subcontinent hosted hundreds
> of thousands of years of stone-tool cultures — the Palaeolithic
> hunter, the Mesolithic fisher, the Neolithic first farmer, and the
> metal-working Chalcolithic villages that seeded the Indus
> civilization.

## 1. What "Prehistory" Means

[definition]
**Prehistory** is the span of the human past before written records.
Its only witnesses are material: stone tools, bones, ash, pottery,
and rock art. India's prehistory stretches from the first hominin
tools (~2 million years ago in some deposits) to the Harappan script
(c. 3300 BCE onward).
[/definition]

| Age | Tool technology | Rough dates |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Palaeolithic (Old Stone) | Roughly knapped stone — handaxes, choppers | ~2 million – 12,000 BCE |
| Mesolithic (Middle Stone) | Tiny microliths, bows, composite tools | 12,000 – 10,000 BCE onward |
| Neolithic (New Stone) | Polished tools, POTTERY, FARMING | c. 7000 – 1000 BCE (region varies) |
| Chalcolithic (Copper-Stone) | First metals + stone | c. 2000 – 700 BCE |

## 2. The Palaeolithic: Hunters of the Ice Ages

Three phases — Lower (handaxe-makers — *Homo erectus* / archaic
humans), Middle (flake tools, early *Homo sapiens*), Upper (blade
tools, modern humans, the first art).

| Phase | Signature sites |
| --- | --- |
| Lower | **Bori (Maharashtra)**, Soan valley (Pakistan), Hunsgi, Attirampakkam (Tamil Nadu) |
| Middle | Bhimbetka (MP), Nevasa, Luni valley |
| Upper | Bhimbetka shelters, Belan valley (first bone tools) |

[info title="Bhimbetka — a gallery 100,000 years in the making"]
The rock shelters of **Bhimbetka (Raisen, Madhya Pradesh)**, a
UNESCO World Heritage Site, preserve stone tools from the Lower
Palaeolithic onward and paintings spanning the ages — hunters
chasing deer, dancing figures, a rhinoceros hunt — in mineral
pigments of red ochre and white. Named after Bhima of the
Mahabharata, the site was reported to science by V. S. Wakankar in
1957–58.
[/info]

Ice ages lowered sea levels (~100 m at glacial maximum), joining
India to Sri Lanka and pushing monsoon patterns around; humans
followed game and gathered tubers, fruits, and honey — a life
reconstructed from burnt seed husks and butchered bones at
habitation floors.

## 3. The Mesolithic: Microliths and the Middle Path

Warming climate (~10,000 BCE) shrank grasslands; hunters switched to
smaller, faster game — fish, birds, deer — and crafted **microliths**
(tiny blades mounted with resin onto wood or bone: the first
composite tools). **Mahadaha and Damdama** (UP Ganges plains) reveal
graves with bodies interred with microliths — the beginnings of
ritual. **Bagor (Rajasthan)** and **Adamgarh** show early animal
taming — the dog first, later cattle bones.

## 4. The Neolithic: The Agricultural Revolution

[important]
The Neolithic is the single greatest transformation in human
history: farming. Domesticated plants and animals meant settled
villages, pottery for storage, surplus, population growth — and
eventually cities. In India it arrives regionally, not all at once:
**Mehrgarh (Balochistan, c. 7000 BCE)** leads — wheat and barley,
mud-brick houses, cotton cultivation — as the western outlier of the
Fertile Crescent farming package.
[/important]

| Region | Sites | Distinctive |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Balochistan / NW | **Mehrgarh** (earliest), Kili Gul Mohammad | Wheat-barley, sheep-goat; ancestor of Harappa |
| Kashmir | **Burzahom** | Pit-dwellings, polished tools |
| Belan valley / Vindhya | Koldihwa, Mahagara | Early rice cultivation claims (~6th millennium) |
| Eastern India / Assam-Bengal | Daojali Hading, Kuchai | Joint burials of humans and animals, jadeite |
| South India | **Piklihal, Maski, Brahmagiri**, Utnur | Ash-mound sites, cattle herding, ragi-horsegram |

The classic Neolithic package: **polished stone axes** (to clear
forest), **pottery** (hand-made, then wheel-thrown), **mud/wooden
houses**, **domesticated animals** (cattle, sheep, goats), and
**textiles** (spindle whorls; Mehrgarh's cotton among the world's
earliest).

## 5. The Chalcolithic: Villages of Copper and Stone

Farmers using the first metal (unalloyed copper) but no writing or
cities — overlapping the later Harappan urban phase:

| Culture | Region (state) | Famous for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Ahar–Banas** | Mewar (Rajasthan) | Ahar/Tambavati, white-painted black-and-red ware |
| **Kayatha** | Malwa (MP) | Copper objects, gold beads |
| **Malwa** | Navdatoli, Eran (MP) | Largest Chalcolithic settlement; painted ware |
| **Jorwe** | Maharashtra–North Deccan | **Inamgaon, Daimabad, Chandoli**; ploughed fields, granaries |
| **Savalda, Prabhas, Rangpur** | Gujarat | Distinct painted wares |

Jorwe villages (~1400–700 BCE) had rectangular houses, barley-wheat
and lentil fields, and a striking burial practice: children buried
in two urns mouth-to-mouth under house floors.

[warning]
Chalcolithic farmers knew copper but the metal was too soft for
heavy forest clearance — most still relied on stone tools. Only with
**iron** (c. 1000 BCE onward, the PGW culture) could the Gangetic
forests be cleared at scale, enabling the rise of the Mahajanapadas
— the subject of the Vedic-to-Mauryan lessons.
[/warning]

## 6. How We Know: The Archaeologist's Toolkit

- **Stratigraphy:** deeper layers are older; artefacts are dated by
  their layer's context.
- **Typology:** tool and pot shapes evolve; sequences like Sohanian
  to microlithic to blade-and-triangle track time.
- **Scientific dating:** radiocarbon (organic remains), occasionally
  thermoluminescence (pottery).
- **Ethnographic analogy:** tribal practices (shifting cultivation,
  megalith raising) illuminate ancient lifeways.

[summary]
India's prehistory runs from the Lower Palaeolithic knappers
(Bhimbetka, Attirampakkam) through Mesolithic microlith-makers
(Mahadaha, Bagor) to the Neolithic farming revolution led by
**Mehrgarh c. 7000 BCE** — pottery, polished axes, the first wheat,
barley, and cotton — and the Chalcolithic copper-age village
cultures (Ahar–Banas, Malwa, Jorwe) that overlapped and fed into the
Indus urban world. Every later lesson — Harappan drains, Vedic
pastures, iron-age kingdoms — stands on these stone-age foundations.
[/summary]
